 1.1 Since $T$ is compact and connected, $Q=T/H$ is compact and connected. By [F1], it is a Lie group. It is abelian because $T$ is abelian. It is not a point because $H\subsetneq T$; a connected zero-dimensional Lie group is discrete and hence a point, so $Q$ has dimension $d\geq1$. [A1, F1, F4, given, algebra]
 
-1.2 Let $\mathfrak q=T_eQ$. The exponential map $\exp_Q:\mathfrak q\to Q$ has differential the identity at zero by [F2], so [F3] makes it a diffeomorphism from a neighborhood of zero onto a neighborhood of the identity. Since $Q$ is abelian, $\exp_Q(X+Y)=\exp_Q(X)\exp_Q(Y)$: the product of the one-parameter subgroups with velocities $X,Y$ is a one-parameter subgroup with velocity $X+Y$, and uniqueness of integral curves gives the identity. Thus $\exp_Q(\mathfrak q)$ is a subgroup containing an open identity neighborhood, hence is open. Its other cosets are open, so its complement is open; connectedness of $Q$ gives $\exp_Q(\mathfrak q)=Q$. The kernel $\Lambda=\ker(\exp_Q)$ is discrete because the exponential is injective near zero. [F2, F3, step 1.1]
