-2.1 The linear span of $\Lambda$ is all of $\mathfrak q$. Otherwise the quotient linear map $\mathfrak q\to\mathfrak q/\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb R}\Lambda$ factors through the compact space $Q=\mathfrak q/\Lambda$ and is onto a nonzero real vector space; that is impossible by [F4]. A discrete additive subgroup of a finite-dimensional real vector space that spans it is a lattice. To see this, a discrete subgroup has a neighborhood of zero containing no other subgroup point, so distinct subgroup points are uniformly separated and every bounded subset of it is finite. Choose a shortest nonzero vector $\lambda_1$; the intersection with $\mathbb R\lambda_1$ is $\mathbb Z\lambda_1$, since any smaller positive step would contradict shortestness. If projections of subgroup points to $\lambda_1^\perp$ approach zero, subtract nearest integer multiples of $\lambda_1$; the resulting points lie in a bounded set, hence in a finite set, so zero is isolated in the projected subgroup. The projection remains discrete and spans $\lambda_1^\perp$. Induction on $d$ gives a lattice basis for the projection; lifting that finite basis and adjoining $\lambda_1$ gives a $\mathbb Z$-basis $\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_d$ of $\Lambda$ that is also a real basis of $\mathfrak q$. [F4, step 1.2, algebra]
