-2.2 Define the nonzero real linear functional $\ell:\mathfrak q\to\mathbb R$ by $\ell(\lambda_1)=1$ and $\ell(\lambda_j)=0$ for $2\leq j\leq d$. Then $\ell(\Lambda)\subseteq\mathbb Z$. Define $\chi_Q(\exp_Q X):=\exp(2\pi i\,\ell(X))$. This is well defined because two logarithms differ by an element of $\Lambda$. It is a homomorphism by additivity of both the exponential map on the abelian Lie algebra and $\ell$; it is smooth because the exponential map is a local diffeomorphism near zero and its translates are local diffeomorphisms everywhere. It is nontrivial: $\exp_Q(\lambda_1/2)\ne e$, while $\chi_Q(\exp_Q(\lambda_1/2))=-1$. [F2, step 1.2, step 2.1, algebra]
